message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "BORDER OPTICS: Satellite Verification of India's Vibrant Villages Programme"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
abstract: >-
  An independent satellite-verification study of India's Vibrant Villages
  Programme (VVP-I), a ₹4,800 crore border-area development scheme sanctioned
  across 2,967 villages in five Himalayan border states/union territories.
  Using Sentinel-2 built-up-index (NDBI) and VIIRS night-lights data at 258
  individually geocoded villages, this study tests whether VVP-I's priority
  villages show measurable physical development since programme sanction,
  under two independent compositing windows (full-year and summer-matched)
  as an explicit robustness check rather than trusting a single satellite
  comparison. A full-year composite shows no significant increase in built-up
  area, while a season-matched composite on the same villages shows the
  opposite result — a reversal traced to snow-cover contamination and monsoon
  cloud cover respectively. Night-lights, the more temporally stable proxy,
  shows no confirmed increase under either window. The instability itself is
  reported as the central finding, addressing a parliamentary record showing
  no independent impact assessment has ever been carried out for VVP-I.
keywords:
  - Vibrant Villages Programme
  - border development
  - securitization theory
  - satellite verification
  - NDBI
  - VIIRS night-lights
  - remote sensing
  - robustness testing
  - India
  - geospatial analysis
